{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task is AGENT-TF-01: implement `solve(case)` that filters API calls using the Toolformer methodology.\n\nFrom the candidate artifact description:\n1. For each call: `gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call`\n2. Normalize gain by prediction tokens: `normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens`\n3. Accept if: `parseable == True` AND `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n4. Keep diagnostics in supplied call order\n5. Return `accepted_call_ids` and `diagnostics`\n\nThe `normalized_gain` in diagnostics should be rounded. The description says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" but doesn't specify decimal places. Looking at the threshold of 0.05 and the values, I'll round to a reasonable precision. Actually, let me re-read... \"Return accepted call IDs and rounded per-call diagnostics.\" - I'll round to 4 decimal places seems reasonable, but maybe they just mean standard Python rounding. Let me check the fixture values to determine what makes sense.\n\nActually, looking at the output schema, `normalized_gain` is just `\"type\": \"number\"` - so it can be a float. \"Rounded\" might mean round to some decimal. Let me think about what makes sense contextually. I'll round to 4 decimal places as a reasonable default.\n\nLet me verify with the first fixture case:\n- call0: gain = 1.48911432 - 0.30132987 = 1.18778445, normalized = 1.18778445 / 5 = 0.23755689, parseable=True → accept (\u003e= 0.05)\n- call1: gain = 1.37006381 - 0.10127803 = 1.26878578, normalized = 1.26878578 / 5 = 0.25375716, parseable=True → accept\n- call2: gain = 1.07992348 - 0.04054692 = 1.03937656, normalized = 1.03937656 / 5 = 0.20787531, parseable=True → accept\n- call3: gain = 1.76582941 - 0.57732788 = 1.18850153, normalized = 1.18850153 / 3 = 0.39616718, parseable=True → accept\n- call4: gain = 1.70674574 - 0.40289862 = 1.30384712, normalized = 1.30384712 / 6 = 0.21730785, parseable=True → accept\n- call5: gain = 1.98682122 - 0.89260518 = 1.09421604, normalized = 1.09421604 / 4 = 0.27355401, parseable=False → reject\n\nSo accepted_call_ids = [\"call0\", \"call1\", \"call2\", \"call3\", \"call4\"]\n\nFor the rounding, the description says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". I'll round to 4 decimal places as a sensible default. Let me write the implementation.\n\nThe implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    \n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        prediction_tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        if prediction_tokens \u003c 1:\n            raise ValueError(f\"prediction_tokens must be at least 1 for call {call_id}\")\n        \n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens\n        normalized_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 4)\n        \n        accepted = parseable and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain_rounded,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        })\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nWait, the description mentions \"normalized_weights, stronger_counterfactual, inclusive_threshold, stable_order\" as hard contracts. Let me think about what these mean:\n\n- `normalized_weights`: normalize by prediction tokens ✓\n- `stronger_counterfactual`: the gain is computed as `loss_without - loss_with` (positive means calling is beneficial) ✓\n- `inclusive_threshold`: `\u003e= threshold` (not strictly greater) ✓\n- `stable_order`: maintain source order ✓\n\nThe description also says \"Validate every required field before computing a result\" and \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\"\n\nI should add validation for required fields.\n\nLet me also re-read: \"Normalize gain by at least one prediction token.\" - this might mean we should validate that prediction_tokens \u003e= 1.\n\nFor rounding, the description says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" - let me use round(..., 4) as reasonable precision.\n\nActually wait - maybe they want rounding to match some specific precision. Looking at the values more carefully, a 4-decimal precision seems right.\n\nLet me write the final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required top-level fields\n    if \"threshold\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required field: threshold\")\n    if \"calls\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required field: calls\")\n    \n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    \n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    \n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    \n    for i, call in enumerate(calls):\n        # Validate required call fields\n        for field in (\"call_id\", \"loss_without_call\", \"loss_with_call\", \"prediction_tokens\", \"parseable\"):\n            if field not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Call at index {i} missing required field: {field}\")\n        \n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        prediction_tokens = call[\"prediction_tokens\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        if prediction_tokens \u003c 1:\n            raise ValueError(f\"prediction_tokens must be \u003e= 1 for call {call_id}\")\n        \n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens\n        normalized_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 4)\n        \n        accepted = bool(parseable) and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain_rounded,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        })\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks correct. Let me verify once more with fixture case 000:\n- call0: (1.48911432 - 0.30132987) / 5 = 1.18778445 / 5 = 0.23755689 → round to 4 = 0.2376, parseable=True, 0.2376 \u003e= 0.05 → accepted\n- call5: parseable=False → rejected\n\nFor case 001:\n- call0: (1.36764237 - 0.73067089) / 3 = 0.63697148 / 3 = 0.21232383 → 0.2123, parseable=True → accepted\n- call1: parseable=False → rejected  \n- call2: (1.66268305 - 0.34997262) / 4 = 1.31271043 / 4 = 0.32817761 → 0.3282, parseable=True → accepted\n- call3: parseable=False → rejected\n- call4: (1.71365839 - 0.09495959) / 2 = 1.6186988 / 2 = 0.8093494 → 0.8093, parseable=True → accepted\n- call5: parseable=False → rejected\n\naccepted_call_ids = [\"call0\", \"call2\", \"call4\"]\n\nThis all seems reasonable. Now let me format the response as required - a JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing the Python source as a JSON string.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # Validate required top-level fields\\n    for field in (\\\"threshold\\\", \\\"calls\\\"):\\n        if field not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing required field: {field}\\\")\\n\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n\\n    for i, call in enumerate(calls):\\n        # Validate required call fields\\n        for field in (\\\"call_id\\\", \\\"loss_without_call\\\", \\\"loss_with_call\\\", \\\"prediction_tokens\\\", \\\"parseable\\\"):\\n            if field not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Call at index {i} missing required field: {field}\\\")\\n\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        loss_without = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"]\\n        loss_with = call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        prediction_tokens = call[\\\"prediction_tokens\\\"]\\n        parseable = call[\\\"parseable\\\"]\\n\\n        # Normalize gain by at least one prediction token (hard contract)\\n        if not isinstance(prediction_tokens, (int, float)) or prediction_tokens \u003c 1:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"prediction_tokens must be \u003e= 1 for call {call_id}\\\")\\n\\n        # Stronger counterfactual: gain = loss without call minus loss with call\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n\\n        # Normalize by prediction token count\\n        normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens\\n\\n        # Round per-call diagnostic value\\n        normalized_gain_rounded = round(normalized_gain, 4)\\n\\n        # Inclusive threshold: parseable AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\\n        accepted = bool(parseable) and normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\\n\\n        # Stable order: append in source call order\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain_rounded,\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted\\n        })\\n\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01U4FJM5Z2IIrD9fqacFxMGp","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":2492,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":20,"output_tokens":2578,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2022},"service_tier":"standard"}}